  "editorReview": {
    "text": "The Lisbon Drop excels in its moral geometry, specifically how Ana’s lies to one handler become leverage for another, creating a genuinely taut pressure cooker. The sensory details are equally sharp—the 'faint blue ghost' of fresh ink on a thumb anchors the tension beautifully. However, the structural polish occasionally slips. I noticed jarring continuity gaps between chapters where named entities simply vanish, and the prose suffers from mechanical repetition that distracts from the narrative flow. While the fair-play clueing and Ana’s hollowed-out character arc deliver a satisfying punch, t",
